    "turn_text": "It's very concerning to me because I thought there was a move, a foot that allowed women to excel with women. When you talk about sports, there's no comparison for the average woman versus the average man. And so that's shameful when you see that. And I'm appalled that I don't see more of the women first coming forward and acknowledging that there's a problem here. You know, that gets to the quality of the discussion, not the color of my skin that was so pointed during the civil rights aspect. And when you start looking at the gender changing for young kids, this is problematic. I think Rand Paul hit it very head-on in regards to, you know, children are still maturing. They're trying to figure out life, you know, and so you're allowing them to take an irreversible process. and make that decision there. Particularly when the facts and the protocols show that it's mitigated, you know, in a natural way as they grow older. Very sad, very, very sad. And that's not what the intent was of the equal rights in 1964. It wasn't the intent there. And I find that instead of being inclusionary, we're dictating everything to a small group. And that's really sad.",
